1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,259
On the seventh of May, 2022, the ice-dammed

2
00:00:03,299 --> 00:00:06,424
lake below Shisper glacier broke through.

3
00:00:06,464 --> 00:00:09,588
Its flood tore down the Hassanabad gorge,

4
00:00:09,628 --> 00:00:12,820
and the Karakoram Highway bridge was gone.

5
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:16,710
Across Gilgit-Baltistan and Khyber Pakhtunkhwa,

6
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:20,620
more than three thousand glacial lakes fill every summer.

7
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:25,877
SUPARCO, Pakistan's space agency, tracks a hundred and thirty-one

8
00:00:25,917 --> 00:00:30,534
of them as potentially dangerous, mostly with optical satellites.

9
00:00:30,574 --> 00:00:33,879
But in July it said cloud after the sixteenth

10
00:00:33,919 --> 00:00:37,290
of June made lake changes difficult to assess.

11
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:40,676
Radar doesn't wait for clear skies.

12
00:00:40,716 --> 00:00:44,073
NISAR, the new satellite from NASA and ISRO,

13
00:00:44,113 --> 00:00:48,150
sends L-band microwaves down and listens for the echo.

14
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:52,294
Rough ground scatters the energy back and looks bright.

15
00:00:52,334 --> 00:00:54,604
Calm water reflects it away,

16
00:00:54,644 --> 00:00:57,390
so a lake looks dark, day or night.

17
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:00,643
At Attabad on the third of July,

18
00:01:00,683 --> 00:01:02,928
Sentinel-2 saw mostly cloud.

19
00:01:02,968 --> 00:01:06,960
Three days earlier, NISAR saw the lake as a dark band.

20
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,441
But mountains cast shadows too.

21
00:01:10,481 --> 00:01:14,570
Radar looks sideways, so slopes facing away from it

22
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:18,121
fall into shadow and look as dark as water.

23
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:22,250
SARtale maps those valley walls with the Copernicus

24
00:01:22,290 --> 00:01:26,380
terrain model and leaves them out of its reference.

25
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:31,854
For every pass, SARtale reads a six-kilometre window around the lake

26
00:01:31,894 --> 00:01:36,338
straight from NASA's archive, without downloading the whole file.

27
00:01:36,378 --> 00:01:40,190
It smooths the speckle, and a pixel is water when it is

28
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:44,106
at least three decibels darker than the window's median.

29
00:01:44,146 --> 00:01:46,127
Inside the lake's outline,

30
00:01:46,167 --> 00:01:48,590
those pixels become a water area.

31
00:01:49,430 --> 00:01:52,167
One number can't tell you about change.

32
00:01:52,207 --> 00:01:56,301
So each pass is compared only with earlier passes on the same

33
00:01:56,341 --> 00:02:00,683
orbit track, because a different angle sees the lake differently.

34
00:02:00,723 --> 00:02:04,200
The spread of those passes is the lake's own noise.

35
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:07,905
One pass beyond twice that noise,

36
00:02:07,945 --> 00:02:11,373
or small changes adding up, is a Watch.

37
00:02:11,413 --> 00:02:14,995
Two passes in a row growing are an Alert.

38
00:02:15,035 --> 00:02:18,771
On the ninth of November, Attabad held 4.43

39
00:02:18,811 --> 00:02:23,240
square kilometres, well within its noise: No change.

40
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:30,025
In winter, radar can't tell new ice from open water, so frozen lakes are found

41
00:02:30,065 --> 00:02:35,840
from clear Sentinel-2 images or NASA POWER air temperature, and get no level.

42
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:38,473
We check ourselves too.

43
00:02:38,513 --> 00:02:41,271
Where Sentinel-2 and radar disagree,

44
00:02:41,311 --> 00:02:43,804
a lake is shown as not measured.

45
00:02:43,844 --> 00:02:47,402
And when NASA updated its processing in October,

46
00:02:47,442 --> 00:02:52,000
the site says when a flag began with that update, like Passu's.

47
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:56,874
In the app, every lake gets one plain status, with a map,

48
00:02:56,914 --> 00:03:00,690
a chart of every pass, and a one-page brief to print.

49
00:03:01,430 --> 00:03:03,521
Anyone can request a lake.

50
00:03:03,561 --> 00:03:07,249
A GitHub workflow counts the NISAR passes over it,

51
00:03:07,289 --> 00:03:11,510
and once the team accepts it, it joins the weekly rebuild.

52
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:16,814
Since the eighteenth of June, NISAR has passed over thirty-nine

53
00:03:16,854 --> 00:03:20,885
watched lakes more than eight hundred and thirty times.

54
00:03:20,925 --> 00:03:23,487
This is screening, not a warning.

55
00:03:23,527 --> 00:03:26,890
When clouds close in, the lakes stay in view.
